ng project for the future  thanks to louis patron from untv for  that report  now to a traditional tibetan dance that  has spread its roots across asia the  tsam is a buddhist custom performed in  countries like china india bhutan and  mongolia  there the dance has long been part of  buddhist culture but the communist  regime that ruled the country for nearly  seven decades banned the psalm and other  religious ceremonies so performances  were few and far between nowadays the  tradition's been revived tmtv's george  sangenyav shows us the steps  the mongolian sam dance is a type of  religious mystery play that has its  origins in buddhist ceremonies  its name comes from the tibetan word  cham which means dance  some was originally performed by monks  to pacify angry gods  a performance of this size hasn't  happened for the last 20 years  the dance was outlawed for decades under  communist rule  i'm very happy that this mongolian chum  dance is being performed again in its  entirety  the thumb dance is both a type of active  meditation and a prayer  you could also describe the dance as an  act of shamanic exorcism a buddhist  blessing and a re-energizing experience  the tibetan dance was adopted in  mongolia in the mid 18th century and  performed on particular festivals and  holidays  soon each buddhist monastery developed  its own variation of the dance  tam dance took on its own mongolian  flavor reflecting local traditions and  ways of life  more than 500 monasteries had  established their own sam rituals by  1921  then came the mongolian revolution and  the start of soviet rule  in 1988 the dance was recognized as a  part of mongolian cultural heritage  people began to stage small sam rituals  in 1990 mainly for tourists the dancers  wear exquisite costumes the pointed  boots resemble the mouths of crocodiles  the masks represent deities  both costumes and masks are made from  gold silver copper leather silk satin  and other types of expensive materials  as well 